  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Friday...\n\n1) Unsettled wx pattern this weekend, potentially wet at times later \nSaturday. \n\nAn upper short wave trough currently moving across the Miss Valley \nwill lift NE across the TN Valley tomorrow and exit off the Mid-\nAtlantic coast by mid-day Sunday.  In response, we'll see several \nopportunities for showers and tstms through early Sunday, the first \nof which being widely scattered showers and tstms the rest of today, \na few of which could include an isolated strong or possibly damaging \nwind gust (see SWODY1 for additional details).  \n\nSaturday, particularly later in the day and early night, appears to \nbe the most unsettled part of the weekend as the trough approaches \nand begins moving across. While a shower/tstm will be possible any \ntime Saturday, the most likely time and best coverage will likely be \nlate in the day from mid-afternoon hours through the late evening \nhours. It's worth noting that central NC is included in the Days 1 \nand 2 ERO, so any of the heavier downpours could results in isold \nflooding of the typical spots that are more sensitive to heavy \ndownpours, such as urban and low-lying areas. \n\nWhile the bulk of the prefrontal shower/tstm activity should be to \nour east by daybreak Sunday morning, we can't rule out a few \nadditional showers or tstms assoc with the actual frontal passage \nwhich will occur later Sunday, thus the reason for the ongoing \nelevated PoPs Sunday afternoon; however, coverage and intensity \nshould be much less compared to the previous day.  \n\n2) Mainly dry with temps closer to seasonable values much of next \nweek.  \n\nMost of next week will be dry thanks to an upper ridge that will be \npositioned to our west much of the time and resulting dry northerly \nflow. In addition, the airmass... while still a few deg above \nnormal... will at least be closer to normal with highs each day \ngenerally in the 80s.  It's worth noting that a closed upper trough, \nbroken off from a larger-scale trough moving off the Northeast \nstates early next week, will drift south parallel to the East Coast. \n Today's NWP models forecast it to remain far enough offshore so \nthat our only impact from it will be occasional gusty NE winds at \ntimes during the mid-week period.",
